            <ArticleTitle>Radiofrequency neurotomy for a patient with deep brain stimulators: proposed safety guidelines.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1046-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1526-4637.2009.00686.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To discuss the potential risks inherent to performing radiofrequency procedures in patients with deep brain neurostimulators, and to propose safety guidelines.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Tertiary care teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 67-year-old male with intractable back pain due to advanced lumbar spondylosis, with a history of advanced Parkinson's Disease requiring two deep brain stimulators.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Radiofrequency neurotomy lumbar facet joints.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">No atypical symptoms were reported during the procedure that would indicate iatrogenic injury from radiofrequency effect on the deep brain neurostimulators. The subject's back pain was relieved by 70% for greater than 6 months bilaterally.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There are several theoretical concerns when using radiofrequency therapies on patients with neurostimulators. Our patient did not experience any known adverse events during or subsequent to the procedure. This article presents our proposed safety guidelines for using radiofrequency neurotomy on patients with deep brain stimulators.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Radiofrequency medial branch neurotomy was performed on a patient with two deep brain stimulators with a satisfactory clinical outcome, and no adverse sequelae. Additional study is warranted regarding the safety and compatibility of brain neurostimulators and radiofrequency interventions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
